      "math_academy_id": "e-11028",
      "problem": "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y) =x^2y\\ln{x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =y\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =y\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})) \\\\  & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =xy(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
